<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01911</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c1429bef-4313-45aa-8d84-9274d6e35ff6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="597b339f-936c-4b64-9099-5504f85227c6" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--q1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="262d2f52-e31f-47b2-b66a-60f84a626b00" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((7,0),(-3,0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((7,0),(-3,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-7,0),(3,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,7),(0,-3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,-7),(0,3)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A quadratic function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=(x-7)(x+3)\).</para>
      <para> Without graphing, identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of \(f\).</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((7,0)\) and \((-3,0)\). If \(x\) is \(7\), \((7-7)(7+3)\) gives (0,10), which is 0. If \(x\) is -3, \((-3-7)(-3+3)\) gives (-10,0), which is 0. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para> Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.1–7.10.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f0164bac-d1a1-4b0c-b157-a5fc12ce0d62" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-21">
    <problem>
      <para> Use the standard form of \((x-7)(x+3)\) to identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\). </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! – 21 The \(y\)-intercept of the graph of the equation is \((0,-21)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.1–7.10.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="55ce37f4-0387-4fe9-b5ef-28c37e3645c7" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="4">
    <problem>
      <para>Answer these questions for the equation \(y=x^2-5x+4\).</para>
      <para> What is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of this equation’s graph? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 4 The \(y\)-intercept of the graph of the equation is (0,4).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 and 7.10.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="68bfe074-e46a-4486-b055-66aa9b00bc06" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((1,0),(4,0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((1,0),(4,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1,0),(-4,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,1),(0,4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,-1),(0,-4)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> An equivalent way to write this equation is \(y=(x-4)(x-1)\). What are the \(x\)-intercepts of this equation’s graph?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((1,0)\) and \((4,0)\) . If \(x\) is 1, \((1-4)(1-1)\) gives \((-3) (0)\) which is 0. If \(x\) is 4, \((4-4)(4-1)\) gives \((0) (3)\) which is 0.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 and 7.10.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4d4d9630-91aa-4245-94f5-1a8c6d8fab42" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Noah said that if we graph \(y=(x-1)(x+6)\), the \(x\)-intercepts will be at \((1,0)\) and \((-6,0)\). Is Noah correct?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! We can check by substituting 1 for \(x\) and -6 for \(x\) and see if they produce an output of 0. If \(x\) is 1, \((1-1)(1+6)\) gives (0)(7), which is 0. If \(x\) is – 6, \((-6-1)(-6+6)\) gives (-7)(0), which is 0.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2–7.10.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f272ae94-b434-4557-98ef-c4fcb2d3a5a9" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;-1&quot;, &quot;3&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;1&quot;, &quot;-1&quot;, &quot;3&quot;, &quot;-3&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> \(x\)-intercepts of this graph.<image alt="A curve in an x y plane, origin O, with grid. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 6, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 8 to 8, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 1 comma 0, 1 comma negative 4, and 3 comma 0." height="195" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6996d5bd6378e1a9f6b8fd7c0df4baa550e2ff48" width="268"/>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at \((-1,0)\) and \((3,0)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 and 7.10.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3477c014-4164-47c2-a96d-60d6aefb0a20" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="8">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the standard form of \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\) to identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph. </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para> Try again. Revisit activity 7.10.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
